#{[17592186477060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477052 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477060 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n2kw=8w+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f4b8651-2348-5ff7-936e-cae0fa3ecd4e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477060 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2kw & =8w+6 \\\\ 2kw-8w & =8w+6-8w \\\\ 2kw-8w & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2k-8)w=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(2k-8)}_{⏟}}_{a}w={{6}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2k-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477053 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477062 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7px=5x+9\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0f885fc-0739-508a-bfee-6d9f76213a02" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477062 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7px & =5x+9 \\\\ 7px-5x & =5x+9-5x \\\\ 7px-5x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(7p-5)x=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(7p-5)}_{⏟}}_{a}x={{9}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p-5 & =0 \\\\ 7p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 7p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{7p}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477073 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477066 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n4qu+4=9u-8\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7b7776b-e31d-5294-a559-386b1ec0c77e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477066 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $9u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu+4 & =9u-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =9u-8-9u \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu-9u+4 & =-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4-4 & =-8-4 \\\\ 4qu-9u & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(4q-9)u=-12.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(4q-9)}_{⏟}}_{a}u={{-12}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4q-9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4q-9 & =0 \\\\ 4q-9+9 & =0+9 \\\\ 4q & =9 \\\\ \\frac{4q}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ q & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477074 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186204092.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477074 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "b2b16748-3249-59b8-8679-78daaba7b541" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477089 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{2}{5}x + 2$ and $y = mx + 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4976d972-daca-5ee7-9f71-69b46357d139" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x+2$ is $\\frac{2}{5}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+9$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{2}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477082 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{12}{5}x - 3$ and $y = mx - 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab1aa788-0fc2-5c61-9a77-7c9bf36f472b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{12}{5}x-3$ is $\\frac{12}{5}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx-9$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{12}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477081 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{7}{9}x + 8$ and $y = mx - 2$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af16e858-af23-50a8-8659-81ccfff82acf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{7}{9}x+8$ is $\\frac{7}{9}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx-2$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{7}{9}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477076 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{3}{8}x + 3$ and $y = mx + 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4217e45-b7c2-59c9-8002-88e6fed81360" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{3}{8}x+3$ is $\\frac{3}{8}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+9$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{3}{8}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477075 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{5}{9}x - 3$ and $y = mx + 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d68e52d7-e69b-5cfa-b2c4-2924c200434d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{5}{9}x-3$ is $\\frac{5}{9}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+12$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{5}{9}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477090 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186087686.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477090 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "0c60c388-88bc-57f0-8030-730e499275e6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478521 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $-21x - 12y = 108$ and $kx + 3y = 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04208a31-b2ae-567f-8896-e86342d6ce9d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $-21x - 12y = 108$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-21x - 12y = 108\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -12y = 21x + 108\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = - \\frac{7}{4}x - 9.\n$$\nIts slope is $- \\frac{7}{4}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 3y = 9$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 3y = 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 3y = -kx + 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{3}x + 3.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{3}=- \\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-3$ gives $k=\\frac{21}{4}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478510 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $24x - 10y = 70$ and $kx + 4y = 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "211d09f0-9544-5c60-a27a-1e3939da787d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $24x - 10y = 70$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n24x - 10y = 70\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -10y = -24x + 70\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{12}{5}x - 7.\n$$\nIts slope is $\\frac{12}{5}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 4y = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 4y = 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 4y = -kx + 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{4}x + 3.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{4}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{4}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-4$ gives $k=- \\frac{48}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478522 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = -7x + 0$ and $kx + 9y = -5$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b6bd912-687a-541d-a6c5-b5a879018279" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = -7x + 0$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-7$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 9y = -5$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 9y = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 9y = -kx - 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{9}x - \\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{9}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{9}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-9$ gives $k=63$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478512 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $x - 4y = 48$ and $kx - 4y = 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dafc1fb-015c-56ff-bc14-52effb959f64" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $x - 4y = 48$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nx - 4y = 48\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -4y = -x + 48\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{1}{4}x - 12.\n$$\nIts slope is $\\frac{1}{4}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 4y = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 4y = 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -4y = -kx + 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{4}x - 3.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{4}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$ gives $k=1$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478505 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = 3x + 6$ and $kx - 6y = -9$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72dbd957-6f59-540e-9ca7-2b36673b5f34" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = 3x + 6$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $3$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 6y = -9$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 6y = -9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -6y = -kx - 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{6}x + \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{6}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{6}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $6$ gives $k=18$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478527 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478516 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $14x - 4y = -36$ and $kx - 7y = 0$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "889f6a39-9b44-51a3-bbee-eb6f0f8b4ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478516 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $14x - 4y = -36$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n14x - 4y = -36\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -4y = -14x - 36\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{7}{2}x + 9.\n$$\nIts slope is $\\frac{7}{2}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 7y = 0$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 7y = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -7y = -kx + 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{7}x + 0.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{7}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{7}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$ gives $k=\\frac{49}{2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478513 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478518 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = 5x + 12$ and $kx - 9y = 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f6b69ef-4822-5ea0-bc5a-d5e5650a7779" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478518 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = 5x + 12$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $5$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 9y = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 9y = 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -9y = -kx + 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{9}x - \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{9}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{9}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $9$ gives $k=45$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478506 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478519 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $-11x - 4y = 40$ and $kx + 5y = -11$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fb31242-a4bd-58a8-a20e-be804320d787" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478519 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $-11x - 4y = 40$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-11x - 4y = 40\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -4y = 11x + 40\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = - \\frac{11}{4}x - 10.\n$$\nIts slope is $- \\frac{11}{4}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 5y = -11$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 5y = -11\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 5y = -kx - 11\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{5}x - \\frac{11}{5}.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{5}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{5}=- \\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-5$ gives $k=\\frac{55}{4}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478509 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478523 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = -12x + 6$ and $kx - 6y = -12$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a61ea21a-7ad8-549d-9c90-07bcff7c3c3a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478523 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = -12x + 6$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-12$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 6y = -12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 6y = -12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -6y = -kx - 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{6}x + 2.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{6}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{6}=-12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $6$ gives $k=-72$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478517 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478526 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = 2x + 2$ and $kx - 5y = 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c691dbfe-adde-5dd9-8252-c74d417e06a8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478526 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = 2x + 2$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 5y = 9$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 5y = 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -5y = -kx + 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{5}x - \\frac{9}{5}.\