frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{6}{5}$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $\\frac{6}{5}$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{6}{5}x + \\frac{1}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479322 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479334 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $9x - 4y = 11$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90eddccf-578f-55f4-af83-c3cc3c505525" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479334 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $9x - 4y = 11$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 4y &= 11 \\\\\n-4y &= -9x + 11 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{9}{4}x - \\frac{11}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{9}{4}$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $\\frac{9}{4}$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{9}{4}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479337 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479338 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479338 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $-5x + 4y = 6$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479338 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa77a9a5-2a4a-5775-aa59-326a7e717119" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479338 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $-5x + 4y = 6$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\n4y &= 5x + 6 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{4}x + \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{5}{4}$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $\\frac{5}{4}$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{5}{4}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479321 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479347 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479347 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $-3x + 4y = 12$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479347 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd7824a2-61a2-52f0-9a18-ecc0098ed2f4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479347 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $-3x + 4y = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 4y &= 12 \\\\\n4y &= 3x + 12 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $\\frac{3}{4}$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{3}{4}x - 18\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479352 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186087687.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186479352 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "7fc258d5-c200-515c-9175-f19a0bd31f45" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502066 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502066 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502066 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Problems Involving Dividing Negative Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502066 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186502129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502066 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5b6c3c0b-b541-5b1f-810b-d9c4ffa1fe9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502067 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502067 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502067 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502067 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186502160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502067 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6d7b67c1-aa22-5106-9f09-19e361dc25c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502068 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502068 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502068 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502068 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186502105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502068 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502069 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502069 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502069 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions and Mixed Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502069 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186502120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502069 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502176 #edb/ref 1061 621937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502176 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a846be7-1beb-5415-afda-c7dbc9dfc761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502176 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502186 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502186 #edb/ref 1061 621932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502186 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71ab7d7f-9cc1-5cdf-9b66-2a332d38a8be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502186 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502187 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502187 #edb/ref 1061 621933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502187 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "75f9f2c1-6398-5c11-a04e-b2c1ba8f749f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502187 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502188 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502188 #edb/ref 1061 621941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502188 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7899018c-d6d5-5a73-8bba-a499b1170d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502188 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502209 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502209 #edb/ref 1061 621978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502209 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f88bb973-cdd9-5dc3-a6f0-ee3f3807638e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502209 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502210 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502210 #edb/ref 1061 621944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502210 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f8abbf23-8275-57f4-b19a-767b1595b9d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502210 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502215 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes we know the total change in a quantity and want to find how much the quantity changes during each equal time period.\n\nIn these situations, we use division.\n\nFor example, suppose a total of $\\[math]24$ is withdrawn from a bank account over $6$ days in equal amounts.\n\nSince money is withdrawn, the total change in the balance is $-\\[/math]24$.\n\nTo find the change each day, we divide the total change by the number of days:\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6 = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balance changes by $-\\[math]4$ each day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502215 #edb/ref 1065 15182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502215 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2472ba13-0b69-56f2-a477-ea1b2a6fc7e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502215 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving Problems Using Division of Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502216 #edb/ref 1064 "Some real-world situations involve a quantity changing by the same amount repeatedly.\n\nWhen the same change occurs repeatedly, we can represent the total change using multiplication.\n\nFor example, suppose a submarine begins at sea level and descends $2\\,\\text{m}$ each minute for $4$ minutes.\n\nSince descending represents a decrease in elevation, the change each minute is $-2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThe total change can be written as repeated subtraction:\n\n![](images/bd/bd6e5218d475c46dd7f0ac578340124954d00fa940c81e32bffa49638b0e79c3.svg)\n\nThis repeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} \\times (-2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ minutes, the submarine's elevation is $-8\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502216 #edb/ref 1065 15164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502216 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e61ac714-2900-508a-9e66-9731f70e9997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502216 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186540116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539465 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1c136286-07e7-54b0-87c2-d0c00e24e994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539536 #edb/ref 1080 "-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1d56b3b6-d2ee-5a6a-ac79-f166c602aad7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539572 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539572 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539572 #edb/ref 108